// of this folder: zeta and L-functions (11M), multiplicative number theory (11N), modular forms and automorphic
// representations (11F), elliptic curves and heights over number fields (11G), and the field theory they need (12E,
// 12F, kept here until there is a folder for field theory). The theorems of algebraic number theory (11R, 11S) and
// Minkowski's convex body theorem moved to ../../algebraic-number-theory/data/theorem-graph.js on 3 November 2026
// (prefix "ant:", same ids).
// A few results whose home class has no folder yet (11C, 11D, 11E) stay here with their own class. In English; built
// on 3 November 2026 from the German file theorems.js of this folder, which stays unchanged for the older pages.
//
// Model (graphs/CLAUDE.md and reports/2026-11-03 Satzgraphen aller Gebiete/auftrag.md): each theorem has `id`, `name`,
// `kind` (key of `kinds`), `area` (five-character MSC class or "basic"), `field`, `msc`, `msc5`, `premises` (the
// theorems that one usual proof, named in `proof`, uses directly), `formula` (quantifier-free LaTeX core), `text`
// (statement), `proof`, `concepts` (ids of data/concept-graph.js, or with prefix of another folder), `year`, `who`.
// Conjectures have no premises and are never premises; conditional proofs and counterexamples of theorems.js are
// left out. References to existing theorems of other folders with prefix: "ent:", "ca:", "ag:", "gt:", "ant:".
// Results from analysis without a home folder have the area "basic".
//
// Sources: Neukirch, Algebraic Number Theory (1999); Milne, Class Field Theory (notes, 2020); Davenport, Multiplicative
// Number Theory (3rd ed. 2000); Iwaniec and Kowalski, Analytic Number Theory (2004); Diamond and Shurman, A First
// Course in Modular Forms (2005); Silverman, The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves (2nd ed. 2009); Darmon, Diamond and
// Taylor, Fermat's Last Theorem (1997); Serre, A Course in Arithmetic (1973); Lang, Algebra (2002).
// Not checked by a specialist.
// Several proofs (user, 3 November 2026): `premises` and `proof` are the standard textbook proof, named in `proofName`
// (optional `proofYear`, `proofWho`); further proofs in `alternativeProofs` ({ name, premises, idea, year, who }), not
// circular and without conjectures as premises. `foundation: true` marks general tools of the area.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory. tests/check-theorems.js warns about theorems without
// premises that have neither mark.

